Ramkumar Ramachandra

I'm a compiler engineer focused on LLVM middle-end optimizations, currently employed at Tenstorrent, and based in London. I previously worked at Codasip and Imagination Technologies. My compiler engineering career started with improving the MATLAB/Simulink compiler at MathWorks, in Massachusetts. A significant portion of my work is [part of LLVM](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/commits?author=artagnon), and I enjoy being part of the community. The desire to be part of an open source community started at the Indian Institute of Technology Kharagpur, my undergraduate school, where I participated in two Summer of Code projects with Git: in the first, I authored [Subversion's svnrdump](https://github.com/apache/subversion/blob/trunk/subversion/svnrdump/svnrdump.c), which facilitates importing/exporting history from a remote SVN server, and in the second, I authored the [Git's sequencer](https://github.com/git/git/blob/master/sequencer.c), which enables `git cherry-pick` to resume after conflicts. The interest in compilers was due to a [coursework project](https://github.com/artagnon/rhine-ml) at Columbia University in New York, where I did my Masters.

I took a career break between my compiler engineering in the US and that in the UK, in Paris, where I fully developed a latent interest in formalized mathematics and logic, and began a [line of research](https://github.com/artagnon/bonak) with a [collaborator](https://pauillac.inria.fr/~herbelin/). I continue working with the research group as a hobby over weekends.

Career wise, I'm always open to talk about opportunities that will let me continue being an active part of the LLVM community. A more formal resume is also [available](/resume.pdf).

This site contains some of my technical and creative writing, and I will admit that I'm an abysmally poor salesman. My writing is meant to be eccentric, fun, and entertaining, with the hope of communicating something useful, and I don't make any attempts to convince, cajole, or sell anything.

